

# Subject: "Area: what the commit does", imperative, ≤ 72 characters, no full stop.
#   e.g. "Galactic Reach: Harbors refit Fleets"   "Viewport check: wait for web fonts"
# Leave one blank line, then the body. Lines starting with # are dropped.
#
# Why: the problem, and why this approach rather than the obvious alternative.
#
# What changes: what a player or caller sees differently; what deliberately does not.
#
# Evidence: the command you ran and its result, e.g. `node scripts/test-rules-contract.cjs` passed.
#
# Trailers, last paragraph, one per line, only when they apply:
#   Refs: #57
#   Security: who can now do what they could not before (or "None:" and why)
#   Breaking-Change: what an ongoing playtest game loses (pairs with playtest-breaking)
#   Co-authored-by: Name <email>
#
# A small, obvious commit needs only the subject. Over ~30 changed lines, say why.
# Fixups: `git commit --fixup=<sha>`, then `git rebase -i --autosquash` before review.
# Set up once per clone: git config commit.template .gitmessage
